WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.803 --> 00:00:07.363
Hi, my name is Baba Shiv.

00:00:07.363 --> 00:00:09.429
I'm a professor of marketing at

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:11.656
the Graduate School of Business.

00:00:11.656 --> 00:00:17.154
[MUSIC]

00:00:17.154 --> 00:00:18.082
&gt;&gt; When it comes to our

00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:19.648
communication, many of us make

00:00:19.648 --> 00:00:21.390
a fundamental mistake.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.600
We start from the wrong place.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.208
We start by saying,

00:00:25.208 --> 00:00:27.287
this is what I want to say,

00:00:27.287 --> 00:00:30.125
rather than thinking about what our

00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:32.122
audience needs to hear.

00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:33.095
&gt;&gt; As it turns out,

00:00:33.095 --> 00:00:35.111
most of our stakeholders are drawn

00:00:35.111 --> 00:00:37.196
to examples, that is why people buy

00:00:37.196 --> 00:00:38.510
lottery tickets.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.659
Even though the actual probability

00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:41.303
of winning

00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:43.170
the lottery is extremely low.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.723
&gt;&gt; There's a famous saying here

00:00:44.723 --> 00:00:46.027
in Silicon Valley that if

00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:47.394
you're a startup founder

00:00:47.394 --> 00:00:48.636
going to an investor for

00:00:48.636 --> 00:00:51.100
money, you're going to get advice.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.054
You go for advice,

00:00:52.054 --> 00:00:54.220
you're going to get money.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.484
So what do you do is that

00:00:55.484 --> 00:00:57.151
go to the stakeholder you're

00:00:57.151 --> 00:00:59.284
trying to influence, you're trying

00:00:59.284 --> 00:01:00.482
to get a buy in from and

00:01:00.482 --> 00:01:02.790
what you do is seek advice.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.680
The person will provide advice.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.885
Now the person's brain has gotten

00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:08.590
invested in your idea.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:09.898
Now your idea will become

00:01:09.898 --> 00:01:11.462
that person's baby as well.

00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:15.143
[MUSIC]

00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:16.082
&gt;&gt; As a leader with

00:01:16.082 --> 00:01:17.456
a coherent strategy,

00:01:17.456 --> 00:01:20.150
you have a method to your madness.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.268
Your strategy is your unique way of

00:01:22.268 --> 00:01:23.660
connecting the dots.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.552
Whenever you ask someone to

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:28.020
accomplish a specific objective,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.159
always help them see how it fits

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:31.812
into the bigger story.

00:01:31.812 --> 00:01:33.042
&gt;&gt; When it comes to making

00:01:33.042 --> 00:01:35.116
decisions about other people, those

00:01:35.116 --> 00:01:37.009
can be immensely consequential.

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:37.954
They can relate to

00:01:37.954 --> 00:01:39.026
their livelihood,

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:40.349
to their sense of worth,

00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:42.437
to their psychological well-being.

00:01:42.437 --> 00:01:45.952
We can't take those lightly.

00:01:45.952 --> 00:01:48.957
[MUSIC]

00:01:48.957 --> 00:01:49.797
&gt;&gt; Here are a few things

00:01:49.797 --> 00:01:50.638
we tell our students

00:01:50.638 --> 00:01:52.580
about how to de risk your humor.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.064
First, don't ask,

00:01:54.064 --> 00:01:56.214
will this make me sound funny?

00:01:56.214 --> 00:01:58.324
Instead, asked how will this make

00:01:58.324 --> 00:01:59.566
other people feel?

00:01:59.566 --> 00:02:01.163
Which also means never punching

00:02:01.163 --> 00:02:02.935
down, that is making fun of someone

00:02:02.935 --> 00:02:04.300
of lower status.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.530
And be sure to check your distance.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.247
How close are you personally to

00:02:07.247 --> 00:02:08.969
what you're making light of.

00:02:08.969 --> 00:02:10.469
I can make fun of my mother,

00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:12.102
but not Jennifer's mother,

00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:13.812
who by the way I hear is a saint.

00:02:13.812 --> 00:02:15.816
&gt;&gt; She is a saint, thank you.

00:02:15.816 --> 00:02:16.608
&gt;&gt; A lovely woman.

00:02:16.608 --> 00:02:18.921
[MUSIC]

00:02:18.921 --> 00:02:21.138
&gt;&gt; My mum once sat in a class and

00:02:21.138 --> 00:02:23.741
at the end of the class she said to

00:02:23.741 --> 00:02:26.442
me, Amir, I have no idea what you

00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:28.864
said, but you're a genius.

00:02:28.864 --> 00:02:31.300
&gt;&gt; Start taking humor seriously.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.952
&gt;&gt; Seriously.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:35.201
&gt;&gt; Seriously, [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; I'm not,

00:02:35.201 --> 00:02:37.200
and I disagree on everything.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.460
&gt;&gt; No, Rob you're wrong,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.260
we don't disagree on everything,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.125
we agree on many things, actually.

00:02:42.125 --> 00:02:47.753
&gt;&gt; Okay, eventually yes,

00:02:47.753 --> 00:02:52.309
we do end up agreeing

00:02:52.309 --> 00:02:56.063
on everything.

00:02:56.063 --> 00:03:06.063
[MUSIC]

